<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Société Générale de Belgique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Société Générale de Belgique was a bank and holding company founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1822 by King William I of the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>. After Belgian independence it financed railways, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines and steelworks, becoming one of the first great investment banks of continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It later held vast interests in the Belgian Congo and dominated much of the Belgian economy until it was taken over by the French group Suez in 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/societe-generale-de-belgique.jpg" alt="Société Générale de Belgique" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boubloub · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brussels_Royale_30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>William I founded the company in 1822 as the Algemeene Nederlandsche Maatschappij ter Begunstiging van de Volksvlijt, the General Netherlands Society for the Advantage of National Industry, partly to administer the large Belgian ducal estates. Contributing 28,108 hectares of forest land, the king was its largest shareholder; the rest was subscribed by public and charitable bodies, companies and individuals. It managed its forests and acted as a bank, issuing its first banknotes on 3 March 1823, and served as the government's fiscal agent through a network of branches. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> branch became a subsidiary, the Banque d'Anvers, in 1827.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> of 1830 it took the name Société Générale de Belgique and sold its lands cheaply to the Board's immediate circle. It remained the country's dominant financial institution after the National Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> was founded in 1850, and has been described as the world's first universal bank. By 1914 it was by far the largest bank in Belgium. During the German occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, with the National Bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, it became formally the central bank through an issue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, while secretly acting on the National Bank's behalf.</p><p>In 1928 it absorbed the Banque d'Outremer and became a major force in the economy of the Belgian Congo. By the end of 1930 it held 32.9 percent of the liabilities of the Belgian banking system. In the 1930s new Belgian legislation required it to spin off its bank as the Générale de Banque, which it kept under its control. In the mid-1960s Time reported that it controlled a fourth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>'s industry and half of the Congo's. After Suez won control in 1988 in a contest with Carlo de Benedetti, Suez acquired all its shares in 1998, and its legal existence ended in 2003 with its merger into Tractebel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Among the companies it once owned were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-miniere-du-haut-katanga">Union Minière du Haut Katanga</a>, Forminière, FN Herstal, the Compagnie Maritime Belge, Electrabel and Tractebel. From 1822 its head office was the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> refuge of Averbode Abbey at 3, rue Montagne du Parc, built in 1779–1781 to designs by Barnabé Guimard and known as the Hôtel Wellington. In 1966 the company and its bank decided to rebuild the whole block, razing Guimard's Petit Béguinage in 1968, and the new Rue Royale front was completed in 1972; the building later passed to Fortis and then to BNP Paribas Fortis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Société Générale de Belgique” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state built a national railway network, opening the line from Brussels to Mechelen in 1835, the first steam passenger railway in continental Europe, and the <b>Société Générale</b> and other banks financed industry at home and abroad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded universities at Ghent and Liège, the <b>Société Générale</b> bank and supported industry such as Cockerill's ironworks.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-kingdom-of-the-netherlands">United Kingdom of the Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
